    "proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2), rho=2*pi-1 and delta=3+pi=epsilon*rho. For a primitive root r=(x,y), q(r) divides 2I(r), with I(r)=(rho*x,(y,T26)). Away from pi, rho and the determinant prime 3, unimodularity excludes every prime divisor of q. At pi the spatial lattice is even and unimodular, so the exponent is zero or two. At rho it is zero or one; exponent one forces y in rho*T26 and is exact by primitivity. At the inert rational prime 3 the Smith form 1^25,3 allows exponent zero or one; exponent one forces x in 3O and both coefficients of y into S3={v:(v,T26) subset 3Z}. The local choices are independent. Class number one and U^+=U^2 give the eight canonical representatives (1 or 2)*(1 or 3+pi)*(1 or 3)."
  },
